WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.468 --> 00:00:02.635
-That feels good, yes,
to have an audience.

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:03.902
-Feels good to have
an audience, finally.

00:00:03.902 --> 00:00:05.538
-I'm so -- We're overdue.

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:08.040
I'm so happy that you're here.
You know I'm a big fan.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:11.411
-No, I couldn't believe
I was invited again.

00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:13.913
Thank you for inviting me.
-Yeah, no, we --

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:17.684
You're one of
the funniest actors I know.

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:20.052
But it's weird. I was just
talking about this earlier,

00:00:20.052 --> 00:00:22.055
is that Molly Shannon
was here, as well,

00:00:22.055 --> 00:00:23.523
and she does dramatic stuff,

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:26.259
and you're a dramatic actor
in this film.

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:28.727
-Yeah.
-Is it tough to be --

00:00:28.727 --> 00:00:31.464
I mean, you're
an uber famous comedian --

00:00:31.464 --> 00:00:33.867
to do -- to have people take you
seriously?

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:35.735
-That's what I want
to talk to you about,

00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:38.404
because I've been doing comedy
my entire life

00:00:38.404 --> 00:00:40.206
in Mexico and Latin America,

00:00:40.206 --> 00:00:42.609
and this is one
of the few dramatic roles

00:00:42.609 --> 00:00:44.109
that I've done in my career.

00:00:44.109 --> 00:00:45.945
And -- But I love it,

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:48.348
and I think I'm going
to switch to drama

00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:49.983
because -- because of that.

00:00:49.983 --> 00:00:53.185
Have you noticed that a lot
of comedians, film comedians,

00:00:53.185 --> 00:00:56.388
they switch at certain point
of their careers to drama

00:00:56.388 --> 00:00:59.792
like Robin Williams --

00:00:59.792 --> 00:01:02.328
-Ray Romano did a great job.
-Tom Hanks, Ray Romano,

00:01:02.328 --> 00:01:04.631
you know, all these guys.
Jim Carrey.

00:01:04.631 --> 00:01:08.301
And I think that it's because --
I hope you can relate to this.

00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:11.170
It's because
people don't take us seriously.

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:13.005
[ Laughter ]

00:01:13.005 --> 00:01:14.907
Not even our kids.
-No.

00:01:14.907 --> 00:01:17.109
No, I am taking you seriously
right now.

00:01:17.109 --> 00:01:18.411
-Thank you.
-I think that you are

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:19.546
really going to do this.

00:01:19.546 --> 00:01:21.314
-You, but these guys don't.

00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:22.515
-No, no, they don't.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:22.515 --> 00:01:24.684
-Anything, anything,
anything we say,

00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:27.020
they think it's a freaking joke.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:28.321
[ Laughter ]

00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:30.022
-Look.
-Yes, that's correct.

00:01:30.022 --> 00:01:31.724
I know, but --

00:01:31.724 --> 00:01:33.693
-Listen, listen, listen. Look.

00:01:33.693 --> 00:01:35.661
-Yes. Yeah.

00:01:35.661 --> 00:01:36.796
-Yellow chair.

00:01:36.796 --> 00:01:38.864
[ Laughter ]

00:01:38.864 --> 00:01:40.299
-Yellow chair got a --

00:01:40.299 --> 00:01:42.001
-Whatever.
Say something, whatever.

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:43.569
-Yeah, I know
what you're talking about.

00:01:43.569 --> 00:01:45.838
Sometimes you'll have
a conversation with someone,

00:01:45.838 --> 00:01:48.808
and I'll go like, "I like this,"
and they go [Chuckles]

00:01:48.808 --> 00:01:50.843
-Yeah, exactly.
-No, I actually do like it.

00:01:50.843 --> 00:01:52.879
-I know, I know.
Funny story, let me tell you.

00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:57.216
I was in Mexico City in a valet
parking lot waiting for my car,

00:01:57.216 --> 00:02:01.587
and the valet parking attendant
brought my car,

00:02:01.587 --> 00:02:03.556
and I saw a new scratch.
-Uh-huh.

00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:07.626
-And I was like,
"Hey. This is a new scratch."

00:02:07.626 --> 00:02:08.962
And he started laughing.

00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:10.696
[ Laughter ]

00:02:10.696 --> 00:02:12.665
And I was like,
"Bro, I'm serious.

00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:14.867
This is new.
Who's going to pay for this?"

00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:16.235
And he laughed even more.

00:02:16.235 --> 00:02:17.603
[ Laughter ]

00:02:17.603 --> 00:02:20.939
So I was like, you know,
"Please call the manager,"

00:02:20.939 --> 00:02:22.975
and he laughed even more!

00:02:22.975 --> 00:02:26.545
The angrier I got,
the more he laughed.

00:02:26.545 --> 00:02:28.348
He thought I was
putting on an act.

00:02:28.348 --> 00:02:29.382
-Yeah.

00:02:29.382 --> 00:02:31.751
-So I just left with my scratch.

00:02:31.751 --> 00:02:32.885
[ Laughter ]

00:02:32.885 --> 00:02:34.387
-But let's talk
about "CODA," though.

00:02:34.387 --> 00:02:37.656
You get an opportunity
to do something like this,

00:02:37.656 --> 00:02:39.692
and how do you even
get the call?

00:02:39.692 --> 00:02:41.427
-Uh, I don't know.

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:42.695
[ Laughter ]

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:45.965
I think it's because
the director is Sian Heder,

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:48.534
an amazing female director,

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:51.905
and she -- She didn't know
I was a comedian.

00:02:51.905 --> 00:02:55.274
She didn't know my background,
and she gave me the opportunity.

00:02:55.274 --> 00:02:56.442
That's good.
-Yes.

00:02:56.442 --> 00:02:58.945
-Imagine you're going to,
I don't know, China.

00:02:58.945 --> 00:03:00.346
-Yeah.
-And another --

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:01.380
The director sees you --

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:02.815
-They don't know me
as a comedian.

00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:04.784
-Exactly. And they're like,
"Oh, this guy is interesting.

00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:06.586
I like his face."
-Yes.

00:03:06.586 --> 00:03:08.454
-And they cast you.
[ Babbling indistinctly ]

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:10.923
And then you got the role.
It's amazing.

00:03:10.923 --> 00:03:13.158
-Can you tell everyone
what the film is about?

00:03:13.158 --> 00:03:15.428
-The film is about a CODA.

00:03:15.428 --> 00:03:18.731
CODA means
a "child of deaf adults."

00:03:18.731 --> 00:03:20.566
And Ruby, who's a CODA,

00:03:20.566 --> 00:03:24.604
is the only hearing member
in her deaf family.

00:03:24.604 --> 00:03:28.107
So she's been translating
for her family her entire life.

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:32.211
She's the only link between the
family and the hearing world.

00:03:32.211 --> 00:03:36.182
So, one day, she joins
the choir club,

00:03:36.182 --> 00:03:38.518
and she discovers
a talent for music.

00:03:38.518 --> 00:03:43.756
And she finds herself torn
between pursuing her dreams

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:47.126
and the fear of abandoning
her family, basically.

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:48.794
-It's amazing, and you are --

00:03:48.794 --> 00:03:49.929
-It's a really cool movie.

00:03:49.929 --> 00:03:51.464
-It's a great movie.

00:03:51.464 --> 00:03:54.033
And it's out now.
You can see it on Apple TV+.

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:55.067
-Yeah.

00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:57.503
-Here's Eugenio Derbez
in "CODA."

00:03:57.503 --> 00:03:58.604
Take a look.

00:03:58.604 --> 00:04:01.640
-Why did you
run out of my class?

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:03.675
-I got scared.

00:04:03.675 --> 00:04:06.278
-Of what? Other kids?

00:04:06.278 --> 00:04:09.848
-Maybe.

00:04:09.848 --> 00:04:12.185
Or maybe finding out
that I'm bad.

00:04:16.588 --> 00:04:19.659
-Do you know what Bowie said
about Bob Dylan?

00:04:21.894 --> 00:04:25.398
"A voice like sand and glue.

00:04:25.398 --> 00:04:29.769
There are plenty of pretty
voices with nothing to say."

00:04:29.769 --> 00:04:33.840
Do you have something to say?

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.175
-I think so.

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:37.676
-Good.

00:04:37.676 --> 00:04:42.481
Then I'll see you in class...
Bob.

00:04:42.481 --> 00:04:43.850
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:47.519
-Eugenio Derbez!

00:04:47.519 --> 00:04:48.954
"CODA" is in theaters

00:04:48.954 --> 00:04:51.591
and streaming
on Apple TV+ right now.

